1. Introduction
---------------

1.1 What is Marshmallow-Assembly?
---------------------------------

Marshmallow-Assembly is a fast, efficient 80x86 assembler that operates in
flat real mode.

It requires at least an 80386 processor to run, but it can assemble programs
for any x86 compatible CPU.

Unlike many other assemblers, Marshmallow-Assembly expects only the information
that is actually needed. The syntax is compact and direct. For basic usage,
see the files in the "examples" directory. For more advanced techniques, see
the "source" and "examples" directories.


1.2 Hardware requirements
-------------------------

Minimum requirements:

- CPU: 80386 or newer
- Memory: at least 1 MB of extended memory is recommended for stable and fast
  assembly

Overall hardware requirements are modest.


1.3 Software and OS requirements
--------------------------------

Marshmallow-Assembly is designed to run on systems compatible with MS-DOS 2.0
or later.

No additional software is required.

It operates in flat real mode. It cannot be run when the CPU is in protected
mode or V86 mode, because entering flat real mode from those states is not
possible.


1.4 Output formats
------------------

Marshmallow-Assembly supports two output formats:

1. Binary (default)
   Produces a non-relocatable flat binary image, suitable for .COM, .SYS,
   and other raw binaries.

2. "format MZ"
   Produces a relocatable, possibly multi-segment DOS MZ .EXE file.

Both formats are generated directly by the assembler. No external linker is
required.


1.5 Output file ordering
------------------------

The output is generated in the same order as the source code.

- For the default binary format, program code or a jump to the entry point
  (for example "jmp start") should appear near the beginning of the source.
- For "format MZ", the entry point is defined with the "entry" directive.
  Segment layout and entry point can be controlled explicitly.


2. Using Marshmallow-Assembly
-----------------------------

2.1 Assembler syntax
--------------------

2.1.1 Instruction syntax
------------------------

2.1.1.1 Data access
-------------------

Data operands follow two simple rules:

    mov eax,myvar       ; place the offset (address) of 'myvar' into EAX
    mov eax,[myvar]     ; place the 32-bit value stored at 'myvar' into EAX

If "myvar" is not defined as a 32-bit value, "mov eax,[myvar]" will cause an
error.

To force the operand size, use an explicit size override:

    mov eax,dword [myvar]
    ; or
    mov eax,d[myvar]

This is dangerous if "myvar" is defined as 8 or 16 bits, because EAX will
receive four bytes starting at the address of "myvar".


2.1.1.2 Jumps and calls
-----------------------

Unconditional jumps and calls:

    jmp  alfa                ; simple jump
    jmp  near byte beta      ; force short jump
    jmp  near dword beta     ; force dword jump
    jmp  10h:50h             ; far jump
    call far pword [1000h]   ; 32-bit far call
    call far dword [delta]   ; 16-bit far call
    call near dword [delta]  ; 32-bit near callb

Conditional jumps:

    je   alfa                ; will be optimized
    jge  byte alfa           ; force byte, error if out of range
    jb   dword alfa          ; force dword, no optimization

The assembler can optimize jump size automatically, or you can enforce a
specific size using "byte" or "dword".


2.1.1.3 Labels and constants
----------------------------

Examples:

    alfa:                         ; simple label
         label     beta           ; equivalent to a simple label
         label     gamma byte     ; low byte of a dword variable 'delta'
    delta          dd 0           ; data label
    epsilon = alfa + 1            ; numeric constant

Local labels:

    sigma:
    .alfa:                        ; local label (1)
         jmp  .alfa               ; jump to (1)
    omega:
    .alfa:                        ; local label (2)
         jmp  .alfa               ; jump to (2)
         jmp  sigma.alfa          ; jump to (1)

Local labels are bound to the nearest preceding non-local label. This keeps
code structured and avoids name collisions.


2.1.1.4 Addresses
-----------------

You can define the size of address values:

    [word 4]          ; generates 0000
    [dword 4]         ; generates 00000000
    [word bx]         ; [bx+0000]
    [byte bx]         ; [bx+00]
    [dword ebx+1]     ; [ebx+00000001]

This gives explicit control over how addresses are encoded.


2.1.1.5 Special cases
---------------------

The "xlat" instruction:

- No argument, or "[bx]" / "byte [bx]" selects the 16-bit form.
- "[ebx]" / "byte [ebx]" selects the 32-bit form.

String instructions can be configured similarly. For example:

    movs byte [es:edi],[fs:esi]

This sets operand sizes and segment overrides. "movs" requires two arguments
in this extended form.

Instructions like "xlatb", "movsb", etc. do not accept arguments.


2.1.2 Number syntax
-------------------

Supported number formats:

- Decimal: 15, 15d
- Binary: 1011b
- Hexadecimal: ABh, 0xAB (case-sensitive)
- Operators: +, -, *, /, mod, not, and, or, xor, shl, shr


2.1.3 Logical expressions syntax
--------------------------------

Logical operators:

- "~" : logical NOT
- "|" : logical OR
- "&" : logical AND

You can define boolean-style symbols:

    true  equ 0=0
    false equ ~true

These can then be used in conditional assembly.


2.1.4 Defining constants
------------------------

"=" works on evaluated values at definition time.
"equ" defines a pure textual substitution.

Example:

    xyz = $

Here "xyz" becomes the address at the point where it is defined.

    xyz equ $

Here "xyz" always expands to "$" and will refer to the address at the point
of use, not the point of definition.


2.1.5 Defining data
-------------------

Initialized data:

    gdtr      db   16, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,0
    attrib    db   0x1E
    col       db   0
    command:  times 127 db 0
    str001    db   'test.bin',0
    argv:     times 10 dd 0

Other data types:

    dw   ; 16-bit word
    dp   ; 48-bit pointer
    dq   ; 64-bit quad word

Reserved (uninitialized) data:

    _proname  rd   1
    _inch     rb   1
    newstack  rd   255
    stacktop  rd   1
    freemem   rd   1

Other reserve directives:

    rw   ; 16-bit word
    rp   ; 48-bit pointer
    rq   ; 64-bit quad word

When using "times" with a label, the label must end with a colon.

Structure-like layouts can be created with macros:

    macro pixel name,x,y,color
     {
      name:
      .x dw x
      .y dw y
      .color db color
     }

    virtual at 0
     pixel pixel,?,?,?
    end virtual

    ; pixel.x, pixel.y and pixel.color offsets are defined

    pixel mypix,10,10,4

    ; now mypix.x, mypix.y, mypix.color variables are also defined

To handle empty parameters, use expressions such as ".x dw x+0" or checks
like "if <x> equ <>".

2.2 Directives
--------------

2.2.1 Preprocessor directives
-----------------------------

include
    Inserts another source file into the current source before assembly.

macro
    Defines macro instructions.

    Syntax:
        macro name arg1,arg2,... {
            ; body
        }

    Arguments may be empty. To check if an argument is empty:

        if <arg> equ <>

    Example macro redefinition of "mov":

        macro mov arg1,arg2
         {
          if (arg1 equ ds | arg1 equ es) & (arg2 equ ds | arg2 equ es)
             push  arg2
             pop   arg1
          else
             mov   arg1,arg2  ; original mov
          end if
         }
        macro mov arg1,arg2,arg3
         {
          if <arg3> equ <>
             mov   arg1,arg2  ; previous macro
          else
             mov   arg1,arg2
             mov   arg2,arg3
          end if
         }
        mov   ax,bx        ; normal mov
        mov   ds,es        ; translated to push es / pop ds
        mov   es,ds,dx     ; translated sequence using both macros

    Excessive macro redefinition in large projects may slow down assembly
    and increase memory usage.

local
    Used inside macros. Declares symbols that are unique for each macro
    expansion.

purge
    Removes the last definition of one or more macros.
    If the macro was not defined, no error is generated.

    To effectively undefine a non-preprocessor symbol:

        macro undefine symbol
         {
          local undefined
          symbol equ undefined
         }

    After "undefine add" you cannot use "add" as an instruction, but you can
    still define a label named "add". If you previously did "_add equ add",
    "_add" still refers to the original instruction.

equ
    Defines a symbolic constant as a textual substitution.


2.2.2 Code settings
-------------------

use16 / use32
    Switch code generation mode to 16-bit or 32-bit. Default is use16.

org
    Sets the origin address at which the code is assumed to be loaded.

format
    Sets the output file format:

    - format binary   ; default
    - format MZ       ; DOS MZ EXE format

label
    Defines a label. Arguments:

    - name
    - optional size
    - optional "at <address>"

segment
    Defines a segment at the current position (MZ format only).
    Code is aligned to a paragraph.
    "use16" or "use32" may follow the segment name.

entry
    Defines the program entry point (MZ format only).
    Argument is "segment:offset".

stack
    Sets stack size (MZ format only).
    If omitted, the default is 2000h bytes.
    May also be specified as "segment:offset".

heap
    Sets additional heap size in paragraphs (MZ format only).
    This is heap space in addition to stack and uninitialized data.
    Use "heap 0" to allocate only the memory strictly required.
    Default heap value is 0FFFFh.


2.2.3 Other directives
----------------------

times
    Repeats an instruction a given number of times.

        times 5 db %           ; generates 01,02,03,04,05
        times 3 times % db %   ; generates 01,01,02,01,02,03

    "%" is the current repeat counter. Nested usage is allowed.

repeat / end repeat
    Multi-line repetition:

        repeat N
          ; instructions
        end repeat

virtual / end virtual
    Opens a virtual block at a given address.

    Data and code in a virtual block are not emitted, but labels and offsets
    defined there can be used elsewhere.

        virtual at <address>
          ; definitions
        end virtual

if / else / else if / end if
    Conditional assembly based on logical expressions. Controls which parts
    of the source are assembled.

end
    Used only as part of:

        end repeat
        end virtual
        end if

    to close the corresponding structures.
